}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'What is embroidery?', a: 'Embroidery is a decoration method that stitches a design directly into fabric using thread. A digital file (the "stitch file") tells a multi-needle embroidery machine where to place each stitch, building up a design out of thousands of individual stitches in different colors. The result is a textured, durable design that\'s woven into the fabric rather than printed on top.' },
 { q: 'How does an embroidery machine work?', a: 'A commercial multi-needle machine clamps the garment into a hoop, then moves the hoop in X/Y axes under a stationary needle that\'s threaded with one of multiple color spools. The machine reads a stitch file and places each stitch in sequence, switching needles automatically when a color change is needed. A 6-head machine does this on six garments simultaneously.' },
 { q: 'What\'s the embroidery process from logo to finished shirt?', a: 'The logo gets digitized, the garment gets hooped with backing, the machine runs the stitch file, and the result gets trimmed and inspected. The design and stitch count drive the timing.' },
 { q: 'How is embroidery different from screen printing?', a: 'Embroidery uses thread while screen printing applies an approved ink system. The exact garment, artwork, placement, quantity, feel, care, setup, and partner determine method fit.' },
 { q: 'What makes a logo suitable for embroidery?', a: 'Before stitching, the artwork, size, garment, backing, and thread get reviewed. Fine details, text, gradients, and effects may require adjustment before approval.' },
 { q: 'Can you embroider on any fabric?', a: 'No. The fabric, the logo, and the placement decide whether it will stitch cleanly. We confirm the method and send a proof for the product you picked.' },
 { q: 'How long has embroidery been around?', a: 'Embroidery has a long history across many cultures. This guide focuses on the current commercial process rather than making a specific historical timeline claim.' },
]

