 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Embroidery stitches your logo into a garment with thread. It looks textured and finished up close, which is why it’s the usual choice for polos, button-downs, hats, and jackets.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="how-it-works" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How Embroidery Works, Step by Step</h2>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Digitize the logo.</strong> The original logo file (vector format ideally) is converted into a stitch file by an embroidery digitizer. The digitizer makes decisions about stitch direction, density, color order, and how detail elements translate. Confirm file ownership and reuse for reorders in writing.</li>
 <li><strong>Hoop the garment.</strong> The garment is clamped into a hoop with stabilizer backing on the underside. The hoop holds the fabric taut so stitches sit flat without puckering.</li>
 <li><strong>Load the stitch file.</strong> The stitch file is loaded into the embroidery machine via USB or network connection.</li>
 <li><strong>Thread the machine.</strong> The needles are threaded with the thread colors needed for the design. A multi-needle machine has 6-15+ needles, each with its own color.</li>
 <li><strong>Run the machine.</strong> The machine places the stitches from the approved file. Stitch count and color changes drive run time.</li>
 <li><strong>Trim and inspect.</strong> After the design completes, jump-stitch threads (threads that connected one part of the design to another) are trimmed by hand and the finished garment is inspected for thread breaks or skipped stitches.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Multi-head machines can decorate several garments in one run. Machine, design, and setup all change the actual output.</p>

 <h2 id="what-makes-it-work" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Makes a Logo Embroider Well</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Not every logo translates cleanly to embroidery. Five characteristics of designs that embroider well:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Bold and distinct elements.</strong> Solid shapes and clear letterforms read crisply at chest-logo sizes.</li>
 <li><strong>Not too much fine detail.</strong> Hairlines and tiny text are the first things to break down.</li>
 <li><strong>A limited color count.</strong> Solid colors stitch cleanly, and each one adds a thread change.</li>
 <li><strong>No gradients or shadows.</strong> Thread is one solid color at a time, so those effects get simplified.</li>
 <li><strong>Readable at the real size.</strong> Check it at chest-logo scale, not on a big screen.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For deeper background, see <a href="/blog/logo-digitizing-101-embroidery/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">logo digitizing 101</a>. The digitizing file is one important part of the result, along with the garment, thread, backing, size, and production settings.</p>

 <h2 id="when-to-choose" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">When to Choose Embroidery Over Screen Printing</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Embroidery is usually the better pick for:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Polos, button-downs, jackets, and hats, where a stitched logo looks at home.</li>
 <li>Smaller orders, since there are no screens to set up.</li>
 <li>Heavier fabrics such as pique, twill, and canvas.</li>
 <li><strong>Simple logos.</strong> Highly detailed art may print better.</li>
 <li><strong>Some protective apparel, if the garment maker allows it.</strong> Check its instructions first, because the wrong thread can affect a rating.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For broader comparison see <a href="/blog/what-is-screen-printing/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">what is screen printing</a> and <a href="/screen-printing-vs-embroidery-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">screen printing vs embroidery</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="costs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Drives Embroidery Cost</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Five factors:</p>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Stitch count</strong>: drives most of the per-piece machine time</li>
 <li><strong>Garment cost</strong>: the blank itself is a meaningful part of the total</li>
 <li><strong>Digitizing:</strong> usually a one-time charge for each new logo</li>
 <li><strong>Decoration complexity</strong>: multiple placements multiply machine operations</li>
 <li><strong>Quantity</strong>: modest volume discount because machine time is fixed per piece</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For full pricing context see <a href="/blog/how-much-to-embroider-a-shirt/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">how much it costs to embroider a shirt</a>, or the polo-specific numbers in <a href="/blog/how-much-to-embroider-a-polo/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">how much it costs to embroider a polo</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="durability" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How Long Does Embroidery Last?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The garment, stitch design, abrasion, and laundering decide how embroidery wears. It does not have the same surface-film failure mode as a print, but threads can still snag, abrade, discolor, or distort.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Dense stitching on stretchy or lightweight fabric can pucker or distort. Review a proof or sample and follow the product-specific care instructions.</p>

 <h2 id="getting-started" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Getting Started With Custom Embroidery</h2>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Pick the garment, or tell us who will wear it and we’ll suggest one.</li>
 <li><strong>Send your logo.</strong> Vector format (.ai, .eps) is best.</li>
 <li><strong>Tell us how many and where the logo goes.</strong> You’ll get the minimum and price in writing.</li>
 <li><strong>Get a digital proof.</strong> A good shop will send you the digitized stitch file rendered before running the machine, so you approve the look before production.</li>
 </ol>

